Why this is Group A standard
This progression mirrors how a professional developer would approach the project:

Data management: Import CSV into a normalised SQLite database rather than repeatedly reading a CSV file.
Software design: Use OOP with clear responsibilities (database access, statistics, modelling, simulation, interface).
Algorithm implementation: Implement the Poisson probability calculation yourself, including the factorial and probability formula.
Simulation: Build Monte Carlo sampling using Python's random module and cumulative probabilities instead of numpy.random.poisson().
Evidence and evaluation: Design experiments to compare approaches (different data windows, simulation counts, and model variations) and analyse their impact.
Testing: Test each component independently before testing the complete system.
This approach demonstrates database design, object-oriented programming, algorithmic thinking, mathematical modelling, and systematic testing—all of which are strong indicators of a high-grade A-level Computer Science NEA.



